From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anton Palgunov <toxblh@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 06:24:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 41/99] v8: e2k: schedule instructions within a straight-line
 window

The instructions since the last point where the code position was asked
are a window of wide commands. Each wide command of an instruction now
goes into the earliest command of the window that its operands, the
registers it overwrites and memory allow, and not earlier than the
measured delays say: a result used too early costs 4 cycles (8 at the
worst), a command that does nothing costs 1, so the user waits and the
commands in between are left for later instructions. The delays (load 3,
multiply 4 to 6, floating point 4 to 6, one more in the other lane of
channels, a 32-bit result read as 64 bits 4) and the store to load rule
are in la_asm.h. That was the reason joining made loops slower: it
brought a floating-point operation one command closer to its user.

A transfer of control stays last, its compare and its target go as
early as they can; a rare branch, an operation on a state register and
predicate logic end the window. A branch is therefore linked to its
label after it has been assembled, by the slot that holds its
displacement, instead of by the position asked before it.

E2K_NO_SCHED=1 gives the old joining, E2K_FUSE_STATS=1 also prints the
lengths of the windows and what ended them.
